## 0. Context you must internalize first

Phases 10–18 made auth, bot persistence, historical/paper/walk-forward/Monte-Carlo backtesting, the scheduled live paper-trading loop, presets/marketplace, Razorpay billing/credits, and all six Layer III intelligence agents (`technical-agent`, `sentiment-agent`, `macro-agent`, `event-agent`, `flow-agent`, `contrarian-agent`) genuinely real — each sharing one hardened `LlmAgentNode` base class for LLM-gateway plumbing (credits, BYOK, audit logging, mode-gating).

**The gap this phase closes:** every Layer IV "ML Predictive Models" component — `gbdt-forecast`, `sequence-model`, `vol-forecast`, `ensemble-stacker`, `anomaly-detector`, `meta-labeler` — is still routed through `apps/engine/app/nodes/universal.py`'s generic `UniversalNode`, specifically the `elif self.layer == "ml":` branch:

```python
pred_return = 0.015 if rsi < 35 else (-0.015 if rsi > 65 else 0.0)
```

This is not a model. It is the same RSI threshold every other unbuilt stub in this codebase reuses, wearing a "12-bar forecast" label. No training ever happens, no model artifact exists, and `predictedReturnPct` is a hardcoded constant (`±1.5%`) regardless of input.

**Your job this phase:** build exactly **one** real, trained ML model — `gbdt-forecast` ("Gradient Boosting Forecast") — end to end: an offline training pipeline that trains on real cached OHLCV + already-real technical/sentiment/order-flow features, a versioned model artifact stored on disk and referenced from Postgres, a real inference node that loads that artifact and produces a genuine probability-based `Signal`, and full integration into the existing audit/credit/mode-gating conventions this project has followed since Phase 14. Do not touch `sequence-model`, `vol-forecast`, `ensemble-stacker`, `anomaly-detector`, or `meta-labeler` — they remain `UniversalNode` stubs. This is a single-model proof, exactly as Phase 14 built one real LLM node (Technical Analyst) before Phase 16 batched three more.

**Critical design decisions, made for you — do not relitigate them:**

1. **Library: LightGBM, not XGBoost, not a deep sequence model.** LightGBM trains fast on CPU (this engine has no GPU), needs no huge dependency tree, handles tabular technical-indicator features well, and — critically — supports probability calibration natively via its classifier API. A sequence model (LSTM/GRU, matching `sequence-model`'s eventual scope) requires a training loop, GPU-friendly tensors, and a much larger engineering surface; that is explicitly out of scope for this phase and is `sequence-model`'s own future phase, not this one.
2. **Training happens offline, via a standalone script, never inside a request/response cycle or the live-tick loop.** This mirrors the project's own existing pattern exactly: `binance_ingest.py` and `seed_macro_events.py` are both standalone, manually-invoked scripts, not HTTP routes. `gbdt-forecast`'s inference node **loads** a pre-trained artifact; it never trains on the fly.
3. **The prediction target is a classification problem, not a raw regression.** Predict `P(price up by more than a threshold in the next N bars)` as a 3-class problem (`up` / `down` / `flat`), not a continuous return percentage. A raw regression on 15-minute crypto returns is dominated by noise and produces a spuriously precise-looking number with no real predictive content; a calibrated 3-class probability is honest about what a tabular GBDT can actually do here, and it maps directly onto the same `direction`/`confidence` `Signal` shape every other agent already produces — no new port type, no frontend change.
4. **No LLM involvement whatsoever in this node.** This is a pure statistical/ML model, not a Layer III reasoning agent. It does not use `LlmAgentNode`, does not touch the credit wallet, does not call any LLM gateway function. Its cost to the user is effectively free compute, exactly like `technical-indicators` or `risk-gate` — this is a deliberate, stated distinction between Layer III (LLM-optional reasoning, credit-metered) and Layer IV (deterministic statistical inference, free).
5. **Historical backtests DO run real inference.** Unlike the Phase 14 LLM-mode-gating rule (LLM calls only in `paper`/`live`), a trained GBDT model is cheap and fast (sub-millisecond inference per row) — there is no cost reason to skip it in `historical`/`walk-forward`/`monte-carlo` modes, and skipping it would defeat the entire purpose of being able to walk-forward validate an ML-driven strategy, which is the whole reason `docs/Full context.md` §9.1's 4-stage validation pipeline exists. `gbdt-forecast` runs its real trained model in **every** mode, always. Do not carry over the LLM's mode-gating pattern here — it does not apply, and copying it would be a mistake, not a safety measure.

