## 4. Task 3 — The inference node

New file `apps/engine/app/nodes/ml/__init__.py` (empty) and `apps/engine/app/nodes/ml/gbdt_forecast.py`:

```python
class GbdtForecastNode:
    component_id = "gbdt-forecast"

    def __init__(self, config: dict = None):
        self.config = config or {}
        self._model = None            # lazy-loaded, see 4.1
        self._model_row = None
        self._load_error: Optional[str] = None

    async def run(self, ctx: NodeContext, config: dict) -> dict:
        ...
```

### 4.1 Lazy, cached model loading — load once, not per bar

Per your Step Zero audit finding #6, confirm exactly when node instances are constructed relative to the per-bar loop. Loading a LightGBM booster from disk takes tens of milliseconds — acceptable once per backtest run, catastrophic if repeated for every one of ~70,000 historical bars. Load the model **once**, on first `run()` call (lazy init, cached on `self._model`), not in `__init__` (which per your audit may run before a DB session is available to query `ml_models` for the active row — if `__init__` already has DB access per the existing pattern, load there instead; **match whatever the existing node-instantiation lifecycle actually supports**, don't assume).

Query `ml_models` for the row where `component_id = 'gbdt-forecast'`, `symbol` matches the bot's configured symbol (from `ctx.candle`), `resolution` matches the bot's configured resolution (available via whatever mechanism `ohlcv-feed`/`live_runner.py`'s `extract_bot_resolution_and_interval` already uses — reuse that, don't reinvent resolution detection), and `is_active = true`. If no such row exists (model never trained for this symbol/resolution combination), set `self._load_error = "No trained gbdt-forecast model found for {symbol}/{resolution} — run app.ml.train_gbdt_forecast first."` and every subsequent `run()` call returns the standard "no data" fallback shape (see 4.3) with this message in `rationale` — never crash the bot's execution loop over a missing model artifact.

If `ctx.db is None` (pure in-memory historical pass with no DB session, per the established Phase 14/17 pattern): mirror the `macro_events_cache` precedent exactly — the model row lookup must happen **once**, upfront, before the per-bar loop starts (in `backtest_runner.py`'s `run_backtest`, alongside where `macro_events_cache` is already fetched), and be passed into `NodeContext` as a new optional field, e.g. `ctx.ml_model_registry_cache: Optional[dict]` (a dict keyed by `f"{component_id}:{symbol}:{resolution}"`, since more than one ML node type may eventually need this same cache). `GbdtForecastNode` checks this cache first if `ctx.db is None`, falls back to a real DB query otherwise — the exact same dual-path discipline `compute_blackout` established in Phase 17. Update `apps/engine/app/nodes/base.py` and thread this new field through `bar_runner.py` and `backtest_runner.py` the same additive, default-`None` way every prior phase's new context field was added.

### 4.2 Building the feature vector at inference time

Using `ctx.historical_window` (the rolling window DataFrame already passed for indicator calculation — confirmed present per your audit) plus the current bar, compute the **exact same** feature set the training script computed (RSI, EMA fast/slow, MACD, MACD signal, zscore, regime-as-a-numeric-encoding — LightGBM needs numeric input, so encode `regime` the same way you did in training, e.g. `1.0` for `"Trend"`, `0.0` for `"Chop"`, and store this encoding convention as a comment in both the training script and this node so they never drift apart). Optionally include `sentimentScore` and `imbalancePct` **only if** an upstream `NewsFeed`/`MarketData`-with-`imbalancePct` output is present in `ctx.upstream_outputs` (reuse `find_upstream`-style lookups) — if the model was trained without these columns (because your Task 2 default training run only used OHLCV-derived features), do not fabricate them; only include columns the loaded model's `feature_columns` list actually expects. **Build the final feature vector strictly in the order given by `self._model_row.feature_columns`** — do not rely on dict insertion order or any implicit ordering; explicitly select and order columns by name from a dict you've built, and raise a clear internal error (caught and turned into a graceful fallback, never an unhandled exception propagating out of `run()`) if any expected column is missing.

### 4.3 Inference and output

```python
probs = self._model.predict([feature_vector])[0]   # [P(down), P(flat), P(up)] per your training-time label encoding
direction_idx = int(probs.argmax())
direction = {0: "short", 1: "flat", 2: "long"}[direction_idx]
confidence = float(probs[direction_idx])
```

Return:
```python
{
    "type": "Signal",
    "direction": direction,
    "confidence": round(confidence, 4),
    "price": current_close,
    "rationale": (
        f"GBDT forecast ({self._model_row.version}, {self._model_row.horizon_bars}-bar horizon): "
        f"P(down)={probs[0]:.0%} P(flat)={probs[1]:.0%} P(up)={probs[2]:.0%}"
    ),
    "audit": {
        "model_version": self._model_row.version,
        "model_symbol": self._model_row.symbol,
        "model_resolution": self._model_row.resolution,
        "horizon_bars": self._model_row.horizon_bars,
        "train_accuracy": float(self._model_row.accuracy) if self._model_row.accuracy is not None else None,
        "probabilities": {"down": round(float(probs[0]), 4), "flat": round(float(probs[1]), 4), "up": round(float(probs[2]), 4)},
        "feature_vector": dict(zip(self._model_row.feature_columns, [round(float(v), 4) for v in feature_vector])),
    },
}
```

The "no model found" / "missing feature" fallback shape must be structurally consistent with every other agent's established "no data" convention (`direction: "flat"`, low confidence, a clear `rationale`, and — new for this node specifically — `audit: {"model_status": "not_found" | "feature_mismatch", ...}` so a bot builder can immediately tell from the trade log why the ML node contributed nothing, rather than silently looking like a real-but-neutral prediction).

